the Xbox Series X launches this holiday  season and ahead of that Microsoft has  dropped a ton of new information on top  of what we already knew about the  next-gen console there's plenty of juicy  new details to comb through and talk  about here's everything we know so far  first new features the Series X uses  quick resume which is an evolution of  the game suspension feature you can  utilize on Xbox one the series X can  simultaneously suspend three or more  games at a time letting you seamlessly  jump between them what's more those  games will load much faster on the new  console versus the x1 you can see the  faster load times in action in this new  video from Microsoft Microsoft is also  introducing what it calls smart delivery  if you own a game on Xbox one you  already own it for the series X all  first party Microsoft titles will  support smart delivery while third-party  developers can make the decision to  include the feature for themselves  we know that halo Infinite and Gears 5  will both support smart delivery and in  Gears case you'll see better textures  and lighting on the series X plus  potential 120 FPS support for  multiplayer the series X will also add  HDR color to games that release without  it and there will be plenty of games  moving into the next generation the  series X is backwards compatible with  all four Xbox consoles the full  backwards compatible library of original  xbox and 360 games currently available  on Xbox one will transfer to series X  and X one games will make the jump two  you can play all those games with the  series X's new controller which is very  similar to the current gamepad but with  small yet important tweaks for one it  actually has a share button it's also  more ergonomic thanks to rounded bumpers  and a d-pad that combines the  traditional cross design with the newer  disc shape the new controller will also  work with the Xbox one and vice versa  you can use your current controllers  with the series X if you wish let's talk  specs Microsoft spilled the beans on  some very technical details the series X  has 12 teraflops of performance and 16  gigs of GDD are 6 memory it's  performance target is 4k resolution at  60fps  up to 120 FPS for its CPU the console  sports 8 zen 2 cores at 3.8 gigahertz as  for storage it includes a one terabyte  custom nvme solid-state drive with USB  3.0 support for external storage you can  hook up your own SSD or HDD but you  won't be able to play any of those  external games unless you use  Microsoft's new proprietary memory cards  that is Microsoft and Seagate have  created external drives that come in  small metal cartridges these use  Microsoft's quote velocity architecture  which lets game developers utilize SSD  memory as RAM Microsoft was apparently  unhappy with how standard SSDs  compromise speed when heated up so it  partnered with Seagate for this external  solution so you can totally store games  on your own drives but they'll need to  be copied to the series X's internal  storage or one of these expansion cards  if you actually want to play them that's  everything we know so far about the  Series X Microsoft is holding a game  stack panel this week so stay tuned a  game spot for any additional information  that comes out of that  you  [Music]
 

